diff --git a/docs/SteamRemotePlay.md b/docs/SteamRemotePlay.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..617ecc0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/SteamRemotePlay.md
@@ -0,0 +1,120 @@
+# Steam Remote Play Together
+
+Eglantine can host a Steam Remote Play Together session: friends watch the stream and play with
+their own controllers as if they were sitting next to you. Each guest's controller becomes the next
+free player, starting at Player 2.
+
+> [!WARNING]
+> This build option links Valve's proprietary Steamworks library into a GPL program. Build it for
+> yourself only: a binary built with it must not be shared or published. Valve's SDK license also
+> forbids committing the SDK to a repository, so it is never downloaded or vendored by the build.
+> Shipping this officially would require a separately licensed process for the Steam integration.
+>
+> You must have an App ID that you own or that its owner has explicitly authorized you to use, and
+> it must be configured for Remote Play Together. This patch does not grant permission to use
+> another game's App ID or Steamworks services.
+
+## How guests reach the emulator
+
+- **Controllers**: Steam turns every guest controller into a virtual gamepad on your PC, which
+  Eglantine reads through SDL like any other controller. SDL hides these gamepads unless Steam
+  started the game, so Eglantine tells SDL to accept them while Steam is connected.
+- **Keyboards**: guest keyboards are kept out of your own keyboard and hotkeys. Each guest keyboard
+  shows up under *Emulation > Configure > Controls > Input Device* as
+  `Remote Play keyboard N (name)`, with the same default keys as the local keyboard. They are not
+  given a player automatically.
+- **Mice**: unused, and their cursors are hidden.
+
+## Building
+
+1. Obtain the Steamworks SDK through access authorized by Valve, accept its license, and download
+   SDK version 1.62 or newer. Unzip it outside the source tree.
+2. Use an App ID that you own or are explicitly authorized to use and that its owner configured for
+   Remote Play Together.
+3. Configure with the option on, pointing at the `sdk` folder and supplying that App ID:
+
+    ```sh
+    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
+        -DENABLE_STEAM_REMOTE_PLAY=ON \
+        -DSTEAMWORKS_SDK_DIR=C:/path/to/steamworks/sdk \
+        -DSTEAM_REMOTE_PLAY_APPID=<your-authorized-app-id>
+    cmake --build build
+    ```
+
+The build copies `steam_api64.dll` (Windows), `libsteam_api.so` (Linux) or `libsteam_api.dylib`
+(macOS) next to the executable. Keep it there, but do not distribute the resulting build.
+
+## Choosing the App ID
+
+Steam shows Eglantine as the game with the App ID it runs as, and gives every controller that
+game's Steam Input layout. The App ID must belong to you or be explicitly authorized by its owner,
+and the application must be configured for Remote Play Together. This feature does not bypass
+Steam entitlement or app configuration checks.
+
+To run as another authorized App ID, open *Multiplayer > Change Steam App ID...*, enter the number,
+then restart Eglantine. The menu entry is there also when Steam refused to connect, so an incorrect
+App ID can be fixed. The choice is saved in `steam_remote_play_appid.txt`, next to your own
+`nx2_switches.txt` (`%APPDATA%\eglantine` on Windows); delete the file to go back to the build
+setting.
+
+When more than one of these is set, the first one wins:
+
+1. The `SteamAppId` environment variable, which Steam sets when it starts Eglantine. A
+   `SteamAppId=<number>` line in `nx2_switches.txt` sets it too.
+2. The App ID chosen in the Multiplayer menu.
+3. A `steam_appid.txt` with the number in the folder you start Eglantine from.
+4. `STEAM_REMOTE_PLAY_APPID` supplied at build time.
+
+## Hosting a session
+
+1. Start Steam and sign in with an account permitted to use the selected App ID.
+2. Start Eglantine directly, not from a Steam library shortcut, unless the authorized application
+   owner instructs otherwise. It connects to Steam on startup; the log says which App ID it runs
+   as. If Steam isn't reachable, the Multiplayer menu shows *Remote Play Together (Steam not
+   connected)*, and the log and *Change Steam App ID...* show the reason.
+3. Open *Multiplayer > Invite Friends to Remote Play Together...* to pick friends in the Steam
+   overlay, or *Multiplayer > Copy Remote Play Guest Link* for a link that anyone can open in Steam
+   or the free Steam Link app. Each link lets one person join. *Invite Anyone to Play* in the
+   friends list of the Steam overlay (Shift+Tab) makes the same kind of link.
+4. The status bar shows the guest count. Hover it to see who is connected and which player each
+   guest controller got.
+
+When a guest leaves, their player gets back the controls it had before. The same happens when you
+close Eglantine, also in the saved configuration.
+
+### Guest controllers do nothing
+
+Steam gives each controller the Steam Input layout configured for the authorized App ID. If a
+controller does not act like a normal gamepad, press Shift+Tab, open *Controller Settings*, and
+choose a *Gamepad* template for every controller listed, yours and each guest's. Steam remembers
+this per controller type. Guest controllers appear in that list only after the guest has joined.
+
+### Your own controller stops working
+
+While Steam is connected, Steam Input can also turn your own controller into a virtual gamepad and
+hide the real one. When every controller Player 1 is mapped to disappears like this, Player 1 gets
+your virtual gamepad, and its own controls back once Steam lets go of the controller or Eglantine
+closes. Turn your controllers on before guests join: a virtual gamepad that appears in the 5
+seconds before the first guest joins, or later, is treated as a guest controller. If Player 1
+still doesn't respond, pick your controller again in *Emulation > Configure > Controls*, or turn
+off Steam Input for it in the Steam overlay menu above.
+
+### More than three guests on Windows
+
+Steam hands guest controllers over through XInput, which has 4 slots, yours included. *Emulation >
+Configure > Controls > Advanced > Enable XInput 8 player support* (restart needed) lets SDL see
+more controllers.
+
+## Testing without a second PC
+
+1. Build and start Eglantine with an authorized App ID as above.
+2. Create a guest link from the Multiplayer menu.
+3. Open the link on a phone or tablet with the Steam Link app, or in Steam on another computer
+   signed in to a second account. Use a controller there, or the touch controls of Steam Link.
+4. Check that the status bar counts one guest, that the guest controller became Player 2 (hover the
+   status bar or open *Emulation > Configure > Controls*), and that Player 2 moves in a local
+   multiplayer game.
+
+If Steam refuses to open the invite or create a link, Eglantine says so. Steam can refuse when the
+overlay is off, the account is not permitted to use the App ID, or the application is not configured
+for Remote Play Together.
